    "result_text": "The full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests green).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the rule that a supplied `registrationDate` later than the server's current date is rejected with HTTP 400.\n\n**What I did:**\n\n1. **Added `RegistrationDateValidator`** (`rest/controller/RegistrationDateValidator.java`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility Spring `Validator` for `OwnerFieldsDto`. It injects the existing `Clock` bean and rejects the `registrationDate` field when it is after `LocalDate.now(clock)`, recording a field error. It deliberately ignores an absent date (that case is defaulted downstream).\n\n2. **Wired it into the controller's binder** (`OwnerRestControllerV1`) \u2014 added it alongside the existing `ownerFieldsValidator` in `@InitBinder`, so a future date surfaces through the same `@Valid`/bad-request path that already yields 400 for other field violations.\n\n**Why this shape:**\n- The existing codebase already establishes two clear patterns: field-level rejections (400) flow through `@InitBinder` validators, while other business outcomes (capacity \u2192 409, daily limit \u2192 429) use explicit checkers in the controller. A future-date field rejection is a field-validation concern, so it belongs with the validator pattern.\n- Rather than growing `OwnerFieldsValidator` (which owns field format/normalization for address/telephone/email/postcode) into a catch-all, I gave the temporal rule its own class. It reuses the existing `Clock` bean, staying consistent with `RegistrationDateAssigner`'s notion of \"server date.\"\n\nThe `errorRejectsFutureDate` acceptance test and the full suite pass.",
